 \begin{abstract}
-Direction normalization retains an observation's direction from a chosen origin and discards its distance. We establish when this compression imposes classification loss across all origins and how one radial bit repairs it. For absolutely continuous binary populations in dimension at least two, directional Bayes risk converges uniformly at distant origins to orthogonal-projection risk. This limit gives an all-origin loss criterion; planar signed-density cores provide explicit lower certificates. For half-annuli of radii $0.8$ and $1.2$ with horizontal separation $1$, a positive loss floor persists throughout the proved disjoint displacement interval $0<h\leq\sqrt{3/5}$ and at the overlapping configuration $h=1$. At $h=0.5$, one binary radius indicator about the midpoint selects between two angular decision rules and restores zero Bayes risk. One bit is the minimum radial addition to exact direction. Controlled neural studies comprise 500 final candidate fits and 250 validation-selected evaluations. In the separate repair experiment, direction-only error is $10.8724\%$; one-bit networks make three errors across 500,000 observations ($0.0006\%$). The results connect a global impossibility criterion, computable loss certificates and decision-preserving recovery.
