 \subsection{Limitations}
-The half-moon results use two-dimensional half-annular supports and exact direction normalization. The general projection-limit theorem covers absolutely continuous binary populations; the explicit global certificates require connected signed-density cores and are conservative lower bounds. The one-bit construction is proved at $h=0.5$ with the specified common radial threshold. Neural inference uses ten replicates per experiment, equal priors, area-uniform sampling and one hidden-layer learner with a fixed training budget. The radius-uniform comparison concerns population risks. Learned excess combines finite-sample, approximation and optimization effects.
