 \section{Conclusion}
-Changing the normalization origin cannot eliminate classification loss for the proved half-moon configurations. The distant-origin limit and signed-density certificates establish this claim over the entire origin plane. At $h=0.5$, retaining one common-threshold radius bit restores zero Bayes risk and achieves the minimum additional radial budget. The paired neural studies measure how learned errors relate to these population limits. Together, the results provide a way to diagnose loss before classifier training and to retain precisely the radial information needed for recovery in the constructed geometry.
