 \section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
-Direction normalization replaces an observation by its direction from a chosen origin, discarding how far away it lies. Observations requiring different labels can then share the same representation. We establish a positive classification-loss floor across all origins for a half-moon family, and prove and test an exact one-bit radial repair. The evidence combines a general limit theorem for distant origins, explicit geometric certificates and controlled neural experiments.
+Direction normalization replaces an observation by its direction from a chosen origin, discarding how far away it lies. Observations requiring different labels can then share the same representation. We give a criterion for unavoidable classification loss over every origin, explicit geometric lower certificates, and a minimal radial repair for a half-moon family. A limit theorem controls distant origins; a support-wide construction and controlled neural experiments establish what the repair retains.
 
-An observation in the half-moon problem is a point whose label identifies one of two crescent-shaped classes (Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry}). Points on the same ray become identical after normalization. Changing the origin changes which points merge, making centering part of the classification problem. We ask whether any origin can preserve the best classification obtainable from raw coordinates and, when recentering fails, how much radial information must be restored. We answer these questions through Bayes risk: the smallest achievable population classification error for a given representation.
+An observation in the half-moon problem is a point whose label identifies one of two crescent-shaped classes (Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry}). Points on the same ray become identical after normalization. Changing the origin changes which points merge, making centering part of the classification problem. We ask whether any origin can preserve the best classification obtainable from raw coordinates and, when recentering fails, whether a single direction-independent radius comparison can restore it. We answer these questions through Bayes risk: the smallest achievable population classification error for a given representation.
 
