 \paragraph{Directions and retained decisions.}
 The spatial-sign transformation retains the direction of a centered observation \citep{Serneels2006}. For $x\ne c$, it is $T_c(x)=(x-c)/\|x-c\|$, where $c$ is the shared origin. For illustration, $(1,1)$ and $(2,2)$ become identical about $(0,0)$; about $(0,1)$ they have distinct directions. Keeping radius $\rho=\|x-c\|$ alongside direction $u$ permits reconstruction through $x=c+\rho u$. Fixed affine standardization is also invertible. These properties supply full-information controls.
 
-The Bayes decision chooses the more probable class at an input. Silva and Narayanan\ \citep{Silva2012} formalize the increase in its error caused by a representation. Silva et al.\ \citep{Silva2024} distinguish retaining all statistical information from retaining enough information for the optimal decision. Their encoder--decoder analysis \citep{Silva2025} connects information measures to prediction losses and controlled synthetic models. For binary classification, a direction map loses decisions when a positive-measure set of rays combines strictly opposite Bayes preferences. This criterion identifies the information that a repair must preserve: the preferred class along each populated direction.
+The Bayes decision chooses the more probable class at an input. Silva and Narayanan\ \citep{Silva2012} analyze representation-induced error; Silva et al.\ \citep{Silva2024} distinguish information sufficiency from preservation of that decision. Gy\"orfi et al.\ \citep{Gyorfi2023} characterize preservation for all loss functions through conditional independence and derive information-based excess-risk upper bounds. Encoder--decoder analysis \citep{Silva2025} also connects information loss to prediction losses. Here a positive-measure set of rays merging strictly opposite Bayes preferences gives positive classification loss. Controlling this cancellation uniformly over origins requires a geometric lower bound, including origins escaping to infinity.
 
 \paragraph{Geometry and origin optimization.}
 Hamsici and Martinez\ \citep{Hamsici2008} optimize projected Bayes error for Gaussian classes with common covariance. Oh\ \citep{Oh2025} learns linear projections using generalized means of pairwise scatter ratios and illustrates how conventional criteria can select a projection with higher Bayes error. Spherical-model analysis compares decision boundaries with Gaussian approximations \citep{Hamsici2007}; normalized-feature support vector machines adjust decision offsets \citep{Graf2003}. These studies connect classification to projected geometry. Here the variable is the common origin of the radial input map, which determines which observations share a direction.
 
 Hurtado et al.\ \citep{Hurtado2001} study wedge separation and compute separating-apex regions for finite planar point sets. A necessary condition is that at least one class's convex hull---the smallest convex region containing it---excludes the other class. Mutual intrusion defeats that condition. We adapt this obstruction to continuous populations and quantify the opposing probability mass merged along rays. Orthogonal projections are already understood geometrically as radial projections from infinity \citep{Orponen2019}. Uniform convergence of their Bayes risks supplies the additional step needed to control origins arbitrarily far from the data.
 
-Poblete et al.\ \citep{Poblete2026} compare four rules on common frozen autoencoder representations across eight datasets. Their radial-compatibility descriptor combines similarity of class variances across principal directions with class separation; its expected directional association with classifier advantage was unsupported. Translating a fixed population relative to a normalization origin preserves class-centered distances while changing its directions. The present analysis uses this controlled geometric relation to separate information discarded by preprocessing from the performance of a fitted decision rule.
+Poblete et al.\ \citep{Poblete2026} compare four decision rules on frozen autoencoder embeddings across eight datasets. Classifier rankings vary, and their class-centered radial-compatibility descriptor relates isotropy and separation to decision assumptions. Translating a population relative to a normalization origin preserves class-centered geometry while changing the directions that coincide. Our population-risk analysis isolates this preprocessing effect from the choice of a fitted classifier.
 
 \paragraph{Magnitude and constrained recovery.}
 Ahmadian and Lindsten\ \citep{Ahmadian2025} contrast a unit-normalized time-series representation with a radial-kernel variant; the variants yield different out-of-distribution ranking performance on two activity datasets. Ding et al.\ \citep{Ding2026} address feature collapse by changing normalization of learned class scores. Magnitude retention in attention \citep{Meng2026}, learned graph centering \citep{Scholkemper2025} and adaptive input transformation \citep{September2024} provide different ways to control what a representation retains. Spatial-sign discriminants use class locations to classify unnormalized observations \citep{Zhuang2026}; their quadratic extension also estimates interclass scale \citep{Shen2025}. These methods establish that the role of magnitude depends on the decision and on where normalization acts.
 
-Classification-aware quantization makes the decision objective explicit: Oehler and Gray\ \citep{OehlerGray1995} incorporate Bayes risk into vector-quantizer design. We retain continuous direction and restrict the added code to a radius comparison with one common threshold. The question is whether this constrained code preserves every population decision. Ni et al.\ \citep{Ni2024} show that repeated affine transformations and normalization can classify arbitrary finite distinct samples. Population preservation instead requires control of angular class densities, which can overlap even when sampled directions are unique. The present contribution connects that population criterion to an all-origin lower bound and a matching one-bit construction at $h=0.5$.
+Oehler and Gray\ \citep{OehlerGray1995} incorporate Bayes risk into vector quantization. Dubois et al.\ \citep{Dubois2021} characterize compression for invariant predictive tasks; with an unrestricted encoder, a single supervised task can be compressed through its predicted label. Our encoder instead retains exact direction and adds only $\mathbf1\{\|x-c\|>t\}$ with one shared threshold. Its scientific content is the geometric sufficiency of this restricted measurement and its certified operating interval. Ni et al.\ \citep{Ni2024} establish finite-sample classification capacity through repeated affine transformations and normalization. The present population criterion controls angular-density overlap, connecting an all-origin impossibility result to an explicit support-wide radial repair.
