 \section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
-The global criterion explains why searching for a better origin can leave an irreducible classification loss. Finite origins merge opposite class preferences along rays, and origins receding in any direction approach a linear projection with its own decision risk. Uniform convergence connects these finite and limiting views into one optimization problem. For connected planar winner regions with mutual convex-hull intrusion, both views lose decisions and a positive global floor follows. This develops the decision-preservation framework of Silva et al.\ \citep{Silva2012,Silva2024} into a criterion for the entire family of moving-origin representations.
+The global criterion explains why searching for a better origin can leave an irreducible classification loss. Finite origins merge opposite class preferences along rays, and origins receding in any direction approach a linear projection with its own decision risk. Uniform convergence connects these finite and limiting views into one optimization problem. For connected planar winner regions with mutual convex-hull intrusion, both views lose decisions and a positive global floor follows. This turns decision-preservation criteria \citep{Silva2012,Silva2024,Gyorfi2023} into a geometric test for a whole family of moving-origin representations.
 
 The half-moon results distinguish the guarantee across origins from the risk at a selected origin. The constructive certificates give explicit lower bounds on the best attainable normalization loss, whereas Table~\ref{tab:neural-main} evaluates the specified origins. At $h\leq0$, a separating strip contains decision-preserving origins. Every fixed positive displacement in the proved disjoint interval has a positive global floor, and at $h=1$ normalization adds loss to existing class overlap. For the original $h=0.5$ midpoint experiment, $10.835\%$ neural error lies about $0.087$ percentage points above the $10.748\%$ population floor. The decomposition locates nearly all of that observed error in the direction representation.
 
-The one-bit construction identifies exactly which discarded information is needed at $h=0.5$. A near/far indicator selects between the two angular rules in Fig.~\ref{fig:bit-repair}. The zero-bit impossibility result and the explicit one-bit decoder jointly establish the minimum additional radial budget. This gives a support-wide construction of decision-aware quantization \citep{OehlerGray1995}: full radius reconstructs the observation, and the coarser binary code preserves its class decision. Under the equal-prior reflected sampling laws, radius has the same distribution in both classes, so the bit alone has $50\%$ Bayes error. Its value arises jointly with direction by selecting the appropriate decision rule.
+The one-bit construction identifies exactly which discarded information is needed at $h=0.5$. A near/far indicator selects between the two angular rules in Fig.~\ref{fig:bit-repair}. The zero-bit impossibility result and the explicit one-bit decoder jointly establish the minimum additional radial budget. The threshold interval proved in the Threshold Addendum shows that this recovery tolerates a range of shared radial cuts. Within decision-aware compression \citep{OehlerGray1995,Dubois2021}, the construction supplies a simple measurement rule: a coarser radial code preserves the decision while full radius reconstructs the observation. Under the equal-prior reflected sampling laws, radius has the same distribution in both classes, so the bit alone has $50\%$ Bayes error. Its value arises jointly with direction by selecting the appropriate decision rule.
 
 The repair experiment then measures how a trained network uses that sufficient input. Its three errors across 500,000 observations coexist with the decoder's zero observed errors and the proved zero population risk. Together with the original paired-origin study, this supplies a controlled benchmark: determine a representation's attainable risk, hold the learning protocol fixed, and measure learned excess separately. Researchers can thereby distinguish a change in retained decision information from a change in how effectively a learner uses it.
