 \paragraph{One bit repairs the disjoint midpoint representation.}
 At $h=0.5$, direction alone has positive loss at every origin. At the midpoint $c=(1/2,1/4)$, append the single radial bit $q=\mathbf1\{\|x-c\|>4/5\}$. For $q=0$, the sign of $9u_1+10u_2$ determines the class; for $q=1$, the sign of $u_2-u_1$ determines it. Positive signs identify class~0. Figure~\ref{fig:bit-repair} shows how the radial bit assigns observations to the two angular rules. Both rules separate the complete class supports, giving zero Bayes risk under either sampling law and any positive priors. Supplementary Section~\ref{supp:one-bit} proves the separating inequalities. Since zero additional radial bits leave the positive direction-only floor, one bit is the minimal radial addition at this geometry.
+
+\paragraph{A certified interval for the same decoder.}
+Replace the radius threshold by $t$, retaining both angular rules. They correctly label the complete supports for every $0.75\leq t\leq0.81$. Proposition~1 of the separate Threshold Addendum derives the maximal interval for this fixed decoder, with endpoints approximately $0.7433034374$ and $0.8120914734$, and proves failure of the decoder outside it. The neural experiments retain $t=0.8$.
